<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canadian–American Reciprocity Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1854</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Elgin–Marcy Treaty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Canadian–American Reciprocity Treaty, also called the Elgin–Marcy Treaty, was signed in 1854 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, on behalf of its North American colonies, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It removed duties on natural products such as grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and fish and opened fisheries and waterways to both sides. Trade grew strongly, but the United States ended the treaty in 1866, which encouraged the colonies towards Confederation.</p></div>
